From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000001 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marcin Niestroj <m.niestroj@grinn-global.com>
Date: Fri, 26 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] rogue_ws: rework "FW started" timeout path to avoid SError
 panic and log safe diagnostics

RGXPostPowerState() polls psRGXFWIfSysInit->bFirmwareStarted with the default
MAX_HW_TIME_US timeout (500 ms on real HW) the first time weston issues
drm_pvr_srvkm_init.

On Astra 1680 SBC the GPU firmware handshake intermittently overruns this window
during cold boot - particularly when userspace bringup is fast (e.g. NFS-rooted
LAVA jobs), where the kernel reaches the poll before the firmware has finished
its first cold start.

When the poll times out the driver reacts in two ways that both bite us:

 1. PVRSRVPollForValueKM() (called with POLL_FLAG_DEBUG_DUMP) issues a
    PVRSRVDebugRequest(), which ends in OSWarnOn(IMG_TRUE). The resulting
    WARNING + Call trace is harmless when the FW comes up shortly after, but
    LAVA's kernel-messages monitor flags it and we get intermittent false
    failures on test jobs.

 2. The post-timeout RGXDumpRGXDebugSummary() / RGXDumpRGXRegisters() and
    RGXCheckFWBootStage() calls read GPU status / SCRATCH registers. The vendor
    comment next to the first two already warns this is "potentially dangerous
    if the reason for not booting is the GPU power is not ON". On the current
    Synaptics SDK (linux_6_12-drivers-synaptics, v2.4.0) the FW boot really can
    fail with the GPU power-domain still off: those reads then raise an
    asynchronous SError (impdef code 0xbf000002, AXI bus fault from the SoC/GPU
    bridge), which arm64 escalates to Kernel panic - 'not syncing: Asynchronous
    SError Interrupt'.

Bumping MAX_HW_TIME_US globally is not acceptable: it gates ~20 other
hot-path polls (HWR recovery, TDM transfer, ...) where a longer timeout
would mask real hardware hangs.

Rework the FW-started path to:
 * poll once quietly (POLL_FLAG_NONE) so a borderline cold boot doesn't trip the
   kernel WARN;
 * on timeout, sleep 100 ms (covers the common "userspace beat the FW" race) and
   re-poll
 * on second timeout, log only fields that live in FW shared DDR memory
   (bFirmwareStarted, ui32MarkerVal, ui32FirmwareStartedTimeStamp,
   ui32InitialCoreClockSpeed, ui32InitialSOCClockSpeed) and return
   PVRSRV_ERROR_TIMEOUT. Skip the register-dump path entirely. The DDR reads
   tell us whether the FW ran at all (markers/timestamps non-zero) or never
   started (everything still zeroed) without ever touching GPU MMIO.

Callers already cope with PVRSRV_ERROR_TIMEOUT. Userspace loses GPU
acceleration, but the kernel stays up and reports the failure deterministically
instead of panicking.

Signed-off-by: Marcin Niestroj <m.niestroj@grinn-global.com>
---
 .../soc/berlin/modules/rogue_ws/src/rgxpower.c | 53 +++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 41 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/synaptics/soc/berlin/modules/rogue_ws/src/rgxpower.c b/drivers/synaptics/soc/berlin/modules/rogue_ws/src/rgxpower.c
--- a/drivers/synaptics/soc/berlin/modules/rogue_ws/src/rgxpower.c
+++ b/drivers/synaptics/soc/berlin/modules/rogue_ws/src/rgxpower.c
@@ -1269,28 +1269,58 @@ PVRSRV_ERROR RGXPostPowerState(PVRSRV_DEVICE_NODE		*psDeviceNode,
 	                         (IMG_UINT32 __iomem *)&psDevInfo->psRGXFWIfSysInit->bFirmwareStarted,
 	                         IMG_TRUE,
 	                         0xFFFFFFFF,
-	                         POLL_FLAG_LOG_ERROR | POLL_FLAG_DEBUG_DUMP,
+	                         POLL_FLAG_NONE,
 	                         RGXFwSharedMemCacheOpExecPfn) != PVRSRV_OK)
 	{
-		PVR_DPF((PVR_DBG_ERROR, "RGXPostPowerState: Polling for 'FW started' flag failed."));
-		eError = PVRSRV_ERROR_TIMEOUT;
+		/*
+		 * Cold-boot FW handshake can overrun the default
+		 * MAX_HW_TIME_US (500 ms) when userspace bringup races
+		 * ahead of GPU firmware startup. Give the firmware a
+		 * 100 ms breather and try once more before treating it
+		 * as a real failure.
+		 *
+		 * Keep both polls quiet (POLL_FLAG_NONE) and skip the
+		 * RGXDump*() / RGXCheckFWBootStage() calls below: on
+		 * this SoC a true FW boot failure leaves the GPU AXI
+		 * bridge in a state where reading status/debug or
+		 * SCRATCH registers raises an asynchronous SError that
+		 * the kernel then turns into a panic. The vendor
+		 * comment already warned the dump is "potentially
+		 * dangerous if the GPU power is not ON" - so log only
+		 * fields that live in FW shared DDR memory and return
+		 * the timeout cleanly.
+		 */
+		OSSleepms(100);
+		RGXFwSharedMemCacheOpValue(psDevInfo->psRGXFWIfSysInit->bFirmwareStarted,
+		                           INVALIDATE);
+		if (PVRSRVPollForValueKM(psDeviceNode,
+		                         (IMG_UINT32 __iomem *)&psDevInfo->psRGXFWIfSysInit->bFirmwareStarted,
+		                         IMG_TRUE,
+		                         0xFFFFFFFF,
+		                         POLL_FLAG_NONE,
+		                         RGXFwSharedMemCacheOpExecPfn) == PVRSRV_OK)
+		{
+			goto fw_started;
+		}
 
-#if defined(TRACK_FW_BOOT)
-		RGXCheckFWBootStage(psDevInfo);
-#endif
+		/* Whole-struct invalidate so all fields read below are fresh. */
+		RGXFwSharedMemCacheOpValue(*psDevInfo->psRGXFWIfSysInit, INVALIDATE);
+		PVR_LOG(("RGXPostPowerState: FW did not start. SysInit shared: "
+		         "bFirmwareStarted=%u ui32MarkerVal=0x%x "
+		         "ui32FirmwareStartedTimeStamp=0x%x "
+		         "ui32InitialCoreClockSpeed=%u ui32InitialSOCClockSpeed=%u",
+		         (unsigned)psDevInfo->psRGXFWIfSysInit->bFirmwareStarted,
+		         psDevInfo->psRGXFWIfSysInit->ui32MarkerVal,
+		         psDevInfo->psRGXFWIfSysInit->ui32FirmwareStartedTimeStamp,
+		         psDevInfo->psRGXFWIfSysInit->ui32InitialCoreClockSpeed,
+		         psDevInfo->psRGXFWIfSysInit->ui32InitialSOCClockSpeed));
 
-		/*
-		* When bFirmwareStarted fails some info may be gained by doing the following
-		* debug dump but unfortunately it could be potentially dangerous if the reason
-		* for not booting is the GPU power is not ON. However, if we have reached this
-		* point the System Layer has returned without errors, we assume the GPU power
-		* is indeed ON.
-		*/
-		RGXDumpRGXDebugSummary(NULL, NULL, psDeviceNode->pvDevice, IMG_TRUE);
-		RGXDumpRGXRegisters(NULL, NULL, psDeviceNode->pvDevice);
+		PVR_DPF((PVR_DBG_ERROR, "RGXPostPowerState: Polling for 'FW started' flag failed."));
+		eError = PVRSRV_ERROR_TIMEOUT;
 
 		PVR_LOG_GOTO_IF_ERROR(eError, "PVRSRVPollForValueKM(bFirmwareStarted)", fail);
 	}
+fw_started:
 
 #if defined(PDUMP)
 	PDUMPCOMMENTWITHFLAGS(psDeviceNode, PDUMP_FLAGS_CONTINUOUS, "Wait for the Firmware to start.");
-- 
2.43.0

